      "title": "New Enterotoxin Malware Uses NPM Packages to Deliver RAT",
      "summary": "The OpenSourceMalware team found two npm packages, clampony and safeclamp3, that impersonate the legitimate clamp utility. Clampony's preinstall hook downloads an obfuscated JavaScript credential stealer that targets AWS, Google Cloud, Azure, Kubernetes, Docker, npm, PyPI, Git and SSH credentials and exfiltrates them via Telegram. That stealer also downloads Enterotoxin, a Windows remote access backdoor that runs Python and batch tasks sent by its server, logs keystrokes, captures clipboard data and screenshots, and persists under Microsoft Store and Windows Update names. Enterotoxin can also rewrite trusted shortcuts so that everyday applications launch the malware first. The safeclamp3 package appears to be a dormant sibling that only runs a placeholder script. Defenders should hunt for these packages and the listed network, file, and persistence artifacts, and rotate any developer or cloud credentials exposed on affected hosts. No actor attribution could be established.",
