      "report_date": "2026-11-24",
      "title": "Vinci RAT Lives in the Microsoft Store",
      "summary": "Vinci RAT is a previously undocumented PowerShell-based remote access trojan tracked by eSentire's Threat Response Unit, delivered through a malicious application named \"Setup Assistant\" published in the Microsoft Store by \"ETPS Ltd\". Victims were redirected from a search for a snipping tool to a fake download site that delivered a Microsoft-signed store installer, which then installed the app. The Electron-based app was obfuscated and acted as a backdoor, executing C2-supplied PowerShell and JavaScript and establishing persistence through a scheduled task. The RAT provides remote and reverse shell access, file staging, and fileless and file-based execution of EXE, DLL, PowerShell, and shellcode payloads, and it disables AMSI. Microsoft reported that the app did not violate its store terms, so defenders should not treat Microsoft Store availability as proof of safety and should restrict store installs and monitor for AMSI bypass and suspicious PowerShell activity.",
